I added the 4 columns: `error_column`, `error_type`, `error_value` and `sample_size`. They sit after `time_unit`, so there are now 33 columns, and `CLAUDE.md` and `metadata.csv` list them in the same order.

**`metadata.csv` rules for the new columns:**
- `error_column`: the name of the time column the error belongs to. If 2 or more time values on one row have errors, the main result goes in the columns and the others in `notes`.
- `error_type`: one of these:
  - `se`, `sd`, `ci95` or `ci<N>`, from the paper's definition;
  - `± not defined` when the paper prints a bare `±`;
  - any other kind as printed, in lower case.

  Never guessed from the value.
- `error_value`:
  - a single number for `se` or `sd`;
  - `lower-upper` for an interval.

  Copied only when the paper prints it, never computed. If the time value is converted to another unit, the error is converted the same way and noted. An error shown only in a figure gets an empty cell and a note.
- `sample_size`: the n for that species, site and fire, as a whole number. If the paper gives only a study-wide n, the cell is empty and the n goes in `notes`.

The updated `metadata.csv` is copied to `data/inference/references/` as well.

**`check.py` errors:**
- an error value without its type or column;
- `error_column` that doesn't name a time column, or names an empty one;
- a CI not written as `lower-upper`;
- an `se` or `sd` that isn't a single number;
- a type or column filled without a value.

It warns on an unusual `error_type` or a `sample_size` that isn't a whole number. I tested it on 3 sample rows: a valid row passes, and a bad column and a bad value are both caught.

`benchmark.py` is unchanged. Your answers have no error columns, so the first run's errors need a manual check against the papers. Knox, Muir and Miller are good ones to check first, since they print CIs.

The old 28-column results are still on disk, and Step 1 will skip every paper already in the LOG. Say when, and I'll check them against the snapshots and clear them.

